The line editing uses a subset of emacs editing keys, familiar to
anyone using unix shells like bash and tcsh.  Where appropriate the
corresponding cursor keys are also supported.

Emacs   Acorn           Windoze         Psion           Action

^A      Home            Home            Psion-Left      Begining of line
^E      End/Copy        End             Psion-Right     End of line
^B      Left            Left            Left            Cursor left
^F      Right           Right           Right           Cursor right
^D                      Delete          Shift-Delete    Delete right
^H      Delete/<-|      <-|             Delete          Delete left
^K                                                      Delete to end of line
^U                                                      Delete entire line
^P      Up              Up              Up              Previous line
^N      Down            Down            Down            Next line

The last two cycle through the command history in a fairly obvious
way.  The command history stores up to 256 bytes of input, which seems
to be enough for typical usage.
